# Keep trybuild compile-fail fixtures LF on every platform so the committed
# expected-error `.stderr` matches on a Windows checkout as well as on Linux CI.
tests/compile_fail/**/*.stderr text eol=lf
tests/compile_fail/**/*.rs    text eol=lf

# The emitted tree is committed so `relearn verify` has something to verify in
# CI. `verify` recomputes each file's body `sha256` and compares it to the hash
# in its own header, so a checkout that rewrote LF to CRLF would hash as
# `HandEdited` and fail on Windows runners while passing on Linux — a break
# visible on one OS only. Pin the bytes at rest rather than teaching the hash to
# forgive them. `[R:pin-eol-for-executable-text]`
skills/**                       text eol=lf
.cursor/rules/**                text eol=lf
.claude/rules/**                text eol=lf
AGENTS.md                       text eol=lf
.github/copilot-instructions.md text eol=lf
copilot-pack/**                 text eol=lf
claude-pack/**                  text eol=lf

# The rule library is the source, and `tests/corpus.rs` asserts every file
# survives a parse/serialize round trip **byte-identically** — the check that
# makes "this format change needs no migration" a fact. The serializer emits LF,
# so a checkout that rewrote LF to CRLF would fail that test on Windows only,
# exactly as the emitted tree would have hashed as `HandEdited`. Same rule, one
# layer earlier. `[R:pin-eol-for-executable-text]`
rules/**                        text eol=lf

# Executable text: a CRLF checkout makes `#!/usr/bin/env bash` unrunnable, so
# makes the interpreter unfindable, so the pre-push gate would arrive disabled
# and fail OPEN. Pin the bytes at rest. `[R:pin-eol-for-executable-text]`
scripts/**   text eol=lf
.githooks/** text eol=lf
